The code tries to use ranged weapons at range, melee weapons
at melee.  In that view it makes sense.  This is a strange case that
reveals a flaw in the logic.

I suspect it's a function of the timing code.

If the gnome lord has a movement first he bumps against the back
of the kobald shaman, realizes he can't melee, and switches to
his ranged weapon.  If the gnome lord has a movement second he
thinks he can advance to melee so he switches to his sword then
promptly bumps into the back of the kolbald shaman.  But having
just moved he doesn't get to switch back.  This is a bug in the
relative movement order between those two individual monsters,
not between their moves and yours.  If they had very different
moving speeds one would get extras and they'd fall out of sync.
As it is, the fact that it doesn't repeat exactly each time suggests
that eventually the gnome lord will get two moves for one by the
kobald shaman and if he's got his bow wielded in the first move
he'll fire an arrow in the second.

It also means he's not out of arrows.  With no arrows both the
bow and sword are only melee weapons and sword beats bow.
He'd switch to sword and keep it if he had no arrows.



